I carried out an analysis of the backend code, using a combination of manual code review and automated tools. Searches were also performed for any unsafe functions
such as eval(), exec() and child_process, as well as hardcoded secrets or suspicious net activity. I also assessed aany dependency vulnerabilities by using npm
audit.

I was unable to find any use of eval(), exec() or child_process in the source code, which indicated that there was no dynamic code execution or
system-level command execution. I also carried out a search for HTTP/HTTPS endpoints, which only identified local development addresses (localhost, 127.0.0.1)
and a GitHub Pages domain (https://tagg02.github.io). These are expected for frontend-backend communication, and no suspicious external connections were found.

There were also references to AES_SECRET, JWT_SECRET, and AWS_SECRET_KEY, but these were accessed via process.env) rather than being hardcoded.
The .env file wasn’t present in the repository and is included in .gitignore so that sensitive data isn’t exposed.

npm audit was able to identify six vulnerabilities, with three being high and the other three being moderate threats.
These affected packages such as multer, express-rate-limit, and path-to-regexp. These vulnerabilities may introduce risks such as denial of service or rate
limiting bypass, but do not show any signs of malicious intent.

The backend does include security packages such as helmet, bcryptjs, and jsonwebtoken, which support secure development practices.

In summary, there was no evidence of malware or any intentionally harmful behaviour.

